================================================================================
Embedded knowledge-layer dependency: @groundfloor/lore
================================================================================

  @groundfloor/lore ..................... Elastic License 2.0 (ELv2)

Atlas embeds a dedicated Lore instance in-process (vendored, not a network
call) — this is Atlas's ONE non-permissive production dependency, and it is a
deliberate choice, not an oversight. Apache-2.0 is not a viral/copyleft
license: an Apache-licensed application embedding an ELv2 library in-process
is not a licensing conflict, the same way any Apache project may depend on a
commercially-licensed library. ELv2 itself principally restricts offering the
licensed software AS A HOSTED SERVICE to third parties and circumventing its
license keys — it does not restrict Atlas's own code, and Atlas's own code
here remains 100% Apache-2.0.

Practical implication for anyone building on Atlas: do not repackage Atlas's
embedded-Lore access as a way to offer Lore's own recall/store/query surface
as a hosted service to others — that would functionally recreate what ELv2
exists to prevent, regardless of Atlas's own license. Atlas's own value is
the code-intelligence and IDE-integration layer (tree-sitter parsing,
cross-file resolution, blast-radius/layer analysis, the MCP surface, curated
semantic writes) built on top of the knowledge layer, not a pass-through to
the knowledge layer itself.

This license is asserted, not just declared here: scripts/check-license-headers.mjs
fails loudly if @groundfloor/lore's own package.json ever reports a license
other than Elastic-2.0, so a future upstream change cannot ship into an
Apache-2.0 release of Atlas unnoticed.

